Roadmap of this fork
====================

Work planned on the 'storage' branch, on top of what CHANGES-FORK lists.
The order inside each section is the order of priority.

Everything here is built on SnapRAID, mergerfs and standard Linux tools,
under GPL-3.0-or-later.


Open items
==========

- Look at the reworked Replace dialog in a browser. Only its rules are tested.
- Guided step for an array left degraded by a recovery whose closing sync
  stopped on a full parity. Today the way out is to free space and then use
  Recover, "accept the disks as they are".
- When a disk is not mounted, name the disk and offer to mount or replace it.
  Never lead with the --force-empty hint.
- Extend the tests of disks of different sizes to xfs and btrfs data disks.
- Test with real multi-TB disks, and the 16 TiB limit of a parity file on ext4.
- Balance: when nothing can be moved because the files are recent, say so
  in the result instead of "Nothing more can be moved".


Features
========

1. Parity swap wizard
   - A new disk larger than the parity becomes the parity, and the old parity
     becomes a data disk, in one guided operation.
   - Offer it from the Replace and Add dialogs when the chosen disk is larger
     than the parity, and from the "parity is full" finding.
   - Keep the old parity untouched until the new one is complete and checked.

2. Cache tier and mover
   - A second set of branches on SSD in front of the pool, written first.
   - Daemon job moving files to the array by age or by fill level, followed
     by a sync. Reuse the engine of the balance.
   - Schedule, manual run, and a per-folder choice to stay on the cache.
   - Warn that files on the cache are not protected by parity until moved.

3. Protection window
   - Done: the Protection and Home pages show the files changed since the
     last sync, its age and the next one.
   - Optional sync when the changes pass a threshold, and after the mover.
   - The added/deleted ratio that lets a sync go on past the delete
     threshold (snapraid-aio-script): the threshold is checked by the
     engine, the daemon would need a diff before the sync.

4. Shares
   - Done: SMB and NFS shares, users, recycle bin, permission reset.
   - Size of every share and the disks holding it.
   - Keep a folder together on one disk (mergerfs "ep" policies), include and
     exclude disks per share. Friendly names of the placement policies on
     the Pool tab.

5. Disk burn-in
   - Done: SMART short and long self-tests, on request and on a schedule.
   - A full surface pass (badblocks) before a new disk is trusted, as a
     background job with a report.

6. Empty a disk, move between disks
   - "Evacuate this disk" to the others of the pool, before removing it.
   - Move a folder to a chosen disk from the file browser.

7. Notification channels
   - Done: ntfy, Discord, Telegram, email and any web address, with a test,
     and pop-ups of the browser.
   - More channels at once, and Web Push without the page open.

8. History of sync and scrub
   - Done: duration and speed of every run on the Protection page, read and
     write history of the disks.
   - Errors found and fixed for every run.

9. Encrypted disks
   - LUKS at format time, unlock at boot with a key file, state in the
     inventory.

10. External disks and remote mounts
    - Done for backups: a USB disk is mounted for the run and released.
    - Mount and eject USB disks with one click.
    - SMB and NFS mounts as sources to copy data into the array.

11. UPS
    - Pause sync and scrub on battery (NUT, apcupsd).

12. Pages for everyday use, what is left
    - Copy of a single file (only folders are copied today).
    - The time window of the maintenance tried end to end in the lab: the
      emulated disks are too fast for a sync still running at a set time.
    - The idle action tried on a real machine: the lab only logs it.
    - A first-run tour of the pages.

13. More arrays, what is left
    - Windows: built from the same sources, but not compiled nor tried.
    - Pools saved in fstab: an explicit order after their branches for
      systemd, on top of "nofail" and of the protected mount points.
    - Per-array metrics in one scrape, with the array as label.


Out of scope
============

- Real-time parity: a different engine from the snapshot parity of SnapRAID.
- Docker, virtual machines, application catalog, remote access service.
- Management of ZFS pools. Detect and show them only.
- Cloud backup: point to restic and rclone.
